"Memory in ChatGPT | OpenAI Help Center (https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-in-chatgpt)\nciteturn559view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-in-chatgpt\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 229\nL0: [Input: Search for articles...]\nL1: \nL2:   1. 【0†All Collections】\nL3:   2. 【1†ChatGPT】\nL4:   3. 【2†Memory】\nL5:   4. Memory in ChatGPT\nL6: \nL7: # Memory in ChatGPT\nL8: \nL9: Learn how ChatGPT uses memory to personalize responses and how to review, change, or remove remembered information.\nL10: \nL11: Updated: 9 days ago\nL12: # Overview\nL13: \nL14: When Memory is enabled, ChatGPT can remember relevant preferences and details from your chats and other available sources. This can help you pick up where you left off and spend less time repeating yourself.\nL15: \nL16: Memory features and controls can vary by plan, region, platform, and workspace settings. If a setting described here is not visible, it may not be available for your account yet.\nL17: \nL18: Depending on your plan and region, those sources can include:\nL19: \nL20:   * past chats\nL21: \nL22:   * saved memories\nL23:   * custom instructions\nL24: \nL25:   * saved files available through Library or Space\nL26: \nL27:   * content from connected apps, such as Gmail\nL28: \nL29: Memory does not retain every detail from every conversation. ChatGPT decides which available information is relevant to a response, and Memory can change as your context changes.\nL30: # Manage memory settings\nL31: \nL32: To review the Memory controls available to your account:\nL33: \nL34:   1. Open Settings.\nL35: \nL36:   2. Select Personalization.\nL37: \nL38:   3. Select Memory.\nL39: \nL40: Depending on your memory experience, available controls may include Memory, Reference saved memories, Reference chat history, Memory summary, Manage, or Saved memories.\nL41: \nL42: For a linked teen account, a parent or guardian can manage supported memory controls through 【3†Parental controls】.\nL43: Turning off Memory does not delete your past chats. If you turn Memory on later, ChatGPT may create new remembered information from chats that remain in your history.\nL44: \nL45: Turning off Memory or personalization does not disable limited safety and security uses of context in rare, high-risk situations. Those uses are separate from personalization controls.\nL46: # Review what ChatGPT remembers\nL47: ## Memory summary\nL48: \nL49: The memory summary is a high-level view of information that ChatGPT may use to personalize responses. It does not necessarily include every detail or source that Memory can reference.\nL50: \nL51: You can also ask ChatGPT what it remembers about you.\nL52: \nL53: When the memory summary is available, you can:\nL54: \nL55:   * Enter a correction in the text field.\nL56: \nL57:   * Highlight text and provide a correction.\nL58: \nL59:   * Select Don't mention this again when that option is available.\nL60:   * Select Delete and turn off memory from the more options menu (•••).\nL61: \nL62: Don't mention this again reduces future references to the information. It does not delete the original source.\nL63: \nL64: Delete and turn off memory deletes the remembered information shown in the summary and turns Memory off. It does not delete past chats.\nL65: \nL66: 【4†Image: Screenshot 2026-06-04 at 8.58.35 AM†images.ctfassets.net】\nL67: ## Sources\nL68: \nL69: When ChatGPT uses relevant personal context, Sources may appear below the response. Sources can help you review information such as a past chat, saved memory, custom instruction, file, or connected email that contributed to personalization.\nL70: \nL71: Sources may not show every factor that shaped a response. Memory Sources are not included when you share a conversation through a shared link.\nL72: \nL73: Depending on the source and available controls, you may be able to:\nL74: \nL75:   * open or review the source\nL76:   * correct or delete a saved memory\nL77: \nL78:   * delete a referenced chat\nL79: \nL80:   * mark a source as relevant or not relevant\nL81: \nL82:   * open a referenced file or email\nL83: \nL84: 【5†Image: Screenshot 2026-06-04 at 8.59.26 AM†images.ctfassets.net】\nL85: # Correct or remove remembered information\nL86: \nL87: ## Correct information\nL88: \nL89: You can tell ChatGPT that remembered information is incorrect or outdated. You can also edit the memory summary or a saved memory when those controls are available.\nL90: \nL91: ## Ask ChatGPT not to mention something\nL92: \nL93: Select Don't mention this again when available, or tell ChatGPT not to use the information in future responses.\nL94: \nL95: This changes personalization behavior but does not delete the underlying chat, file, email, or other source.\nL96: ## Delete remembered information\nL97: \nL98: The required steps depend on where the information is stored.\nL99: \nL100:   * Delete an item from the memory summary or saved-memory controls.\nL101: \nL102:   * Delete the chat where you shared or referenced the information.\nL103: \nL104:   * Delete relevant saved files from Library or Space.\nL105: \nL106:   * Disconnect an app that contains the information when you no longer want ChatGPT to access it.\nL107: \nL108: Deleting a chat alone does not necessarily delete a separate saved memory created from that chat.\nL109: ## Remove remembered information from its sources\nL110: \nL111: To remove information saved as a memory, delete the saved memory and the chat where you first shared it. Also remove the information from any other sources where it appears:\nL112: \nL113:   * the memory summary\nL114: \nL115:   * saved memories\nL116: \nL117:   * regular and archived chats\nL118: \nL119:   * saved files available through Library or Space\nL120: \nL121:   * connected apps\nL122: It can take time for deletion and Memory updates to propagate. OpenAI may retain logs of deleted saved memories for up to 30 days for safety and debugging purposes.\nL123: # Reference chat history\nL124: \nL125: If your settings include Reference chat history, turning it on lets ChatGPT use relevant information from past conversations to personalize future responses.\nL126: \nL127: Unlike an explicit saved memory, information derived from chat history can change as ChatGPT updates what is most useful to remember.\nL128: If you turn Reference chat history off, information remembered from past chats is scheduled for deletion from OpenAI systems within 30 days. The original chats remain in your history unless you delete them.\nL129: \nL130: If your settings include Reference saved memories, turning it off also turns off Reference chat history. When Reference saved memories is on, you can manage Reference chat history separately.\nL131: \nL132: There is no separate storage limit for what ChatGPT can reference through chat history.\nL133: Turning off Reference chat history does not delete your saved memories.\nL134: # Saved memories\nL135: \nL136: Saved memories are details that you explicitly ask ChatGPT to remember or that ChatGPT saves as useful context when that behavior is available.\nL137: \nL138: Saved memories are stored separately from chat history. Deleting the original chat does not automatically delete a separate saved memory.\nL139: You can ask ChatGPT to forget a saved memory or delete it from Memory settings. Deleting a saved memory prevents it from being used in future personalization, but it does not remove mentions of that information from past conversations.\nL140: \nL141: Depending on your plan and platform, saved-memory controls can also include search, sorting, automatic prioritization, and version history.\nL142: # Improved memory and legacy saved memories\nL143: \nL144: Improved Memory continually updates a broader summary of relevant context. Legacy saved memories use a more explicit list of individual memory items.\nL145: \nL146: Where the option is available, you can switch between the experiences:\nL147: \nL148:   1. Open Settings.\nL149: \nL150:   2. Select Personalization.\nL151: \nL152:   3. Select Memory.\nL153: \nL154:   4. Select Saved memories to use the legacy experience, or select Try improved memory to return to improved Memory.\nL155: Changing the Memory experience does not delete your chat history.\nL156: # Improved memory in regulated workspaces\nL157: \nL158: Improved memory can use context from past chats to keep its memory current. It is distinct from Memory generally and from the legacy saved memories system.\nL159: \nL160: In ChatGPT for Healthcare and ChatGPT Enterprise with Regulated Workspace, improved memory is disabled by default. Workspace owners and admins can make Use improved memory available to the default workspace role or eligible custom roles.\nL161: This feature is not covered under your BAA. PHI should not be entered when using this feature.\nL162: Members who choose to use improved memory can use Project-only memory to keep memory context limited to a specific project. Chats can reference other conversations in the same project, but they cannot reference conversations outside the project, and chats outside the project cannot reference conversations in it. Project-only memory changes where context can be referenced; it does not change the BAA guidance in this section.\nL163: \nL164: For setup and availability requirements, see: 【6†Projects in ChatGPT】.\nL165: Before enabling improved memory, workspace owners and admins should review who will receive access and tell affected members about this restriction. If a memory setting is unavailable in an Enterprise workspace, contact your workspace admin.\nL166: \nL167: For the current list of functionality covered and not covered under the BAA, see: 【7†HIPAA Eligible Products and Functionality】.\nL168: # Memory in locked chats\nL169: \nL170: When Memory is enabled, locked chats can use relevant information from regular Memory. New messages sent while a chat is locked do not personalize regular chats.\nL171: \nL172: Locking a conversation does not remove information that ChatGPT remembered before it was locked. After permanent unlock, the full conversation may be used for regular Memory if your settings and workspace policies allow it.\nL173: # Memory in temporary chat\nL174: \nL175: Before starting a temporary chat, you can choose whether it uses existing memories, custom instructions, and plugins. Select Unpersonalized if you do not want to use them. You cannot change this choice after the conversation starts.\nL176: \nL177: Temporary chats do not create or update memories, even with personalization on. Your account and workspace restrictions still apply.\nL178: Saving a temporary chat converts it to a regular chat. It then follows your account’s personalization and model-improvement settings. If Memory is on, ChatGPT may use the saved conversation to personalize future responses.\nL179: \nL180: Limited safety-relevant context may be used in rare, high-risk situations. This safety use is separate from Memory personalization.\nL181: \nL182: For more information about temporary chat, see: 【8†Temporary chat in ChatGPT】.\nL183: # Privacy and model training\nL184: \nL185: Sensitive information can appear in Memory if you share it with ChatGPT. Review Memory settings and Sources, and choose a non-personalized temporary chat when you do not want the conversation to use or create memories for personalization. If you save the chat, it becomes a regular chat and follows your account’s personalization settings.\nL186: For personal ChatGPT accounts, OpenAI may use chats and remembered information to improve models when Improve the model for everyone is on. You can turn that setting off in Data controls.\nL187: \nL188: By default, OpenAI does not use content from ChatGPT Business, Enterprise, Edu, or ChatGPT for Healthcare workspaces to train its models.\nL189: \nL190: For more information about data controls, see: 【9†Data controls in ChatGPT】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChatGPT Work and Codex | OpenAI Help Center (https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex)\nciteturn559view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 207\nL0: [Input: Search for articles...]\nL1: \nL2:   1. 【0†All Collections】\nL3:   2. 【1†ChatGPT】\nL4:   3. ChatGPT Work and Codex\nL5: \nL6: # ChatGPT Work and Codex\nL7: \nL8: Use Chat or Work in ChatGPT, or switch to Codex for software development.\nL9: \nL10: Updated: 4 days ago\nL11: # Overview\nL12: \nL13: GPT‑6 Pro, powered by GPT‑6 Astra, is available in ChatGPT for Pro $100, Pro $200, Business and Enterprise plans. Enterprise access also depends on your workspace’s model-access permissions. Plus plans include GPT‑6 Astra in ChatGPT Work and Codex.\nL14: Note: Astra requires Codex CLI version 0.153.0 or newer. Additionally, please update to the latest available ChatGPT Desktop app. If you do not see Astra available in Chat, Work or Codex in the desktop app, ensure that you update your app by clicking Menu > Check for Updates, even if you recently updated the app due to an automatic update notification or by clicking on the update icon in the sidebar.\nL15: ChatGPT includes Chat and Work. Codex is available in the desktop app, and Codex Cloud lets you start and continue coding tasks from desktop, web, or mobile. Chat is for fast, conversational assistance and everyday questions. Work is an agent designed for longer, multi-step work and finished deliverables. Codex remains dedicated to software development and technical work.\nL16: 【2†ChatGPT Voice】 adds a live voice interface to Work on web and mobile, and to Work and Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app. You can speak, interrupt naturally, and use the tools and permissions available to the selected experience.\nL17: \nL18: Work follows the same usage structure as Codex. The 【3†Codex pricing page†developers.openai.com】 explains included usage and additional credits; its examples are based on coding tasks, so Work usage will vary by task.\nL19: # Models in Work and Codex\nL20: \nL21: GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6.1 Terra, GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna are models for ChatGPT Work and Codex. They are not available in regular ChatGPT conversations. GPT-6.1 Sol is rolling out for eligible paid plans. Availability depends on your plan, workspace settings and rollout access.\nL22: \nL23: GPT-6.1 Sol has a default context window of 500,000 tokens in ChatGPT Work and Codex.\nL24: There’s no additional long-context surcharge for GPT-6.1 Sol usage covered by your subscription’s included allowance. Longer conversations and tasks still count toward your usage limits. Usage paid with purchased credits, Enterprise pay-as-you-go usage, and API usage follow their existing pricing rules.\nL25: Use the model picker to select Default or a specific model. Default combines models and reasoning levels along one slider. Selecting a model lets you choose from that model’s available reasoning levels. The options shown depend on your plan and workspace settings. Codex CLI does not use a slider.\nL26: \nL27: Codex keeps the model you selected manually.\nL28: \nL29: For workspace model access and usage details, see 【4†ChatGPT Business models and limits】 or 【5†ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu - Models & Limits】.\nL30: # Astra usage in Work and Codex\nL31: \nL32: For Astra at Standard speed or in Fast mode, usage draws from your plan’s included Work and Codex allowance. Pro $100 and Pro $200 plans and Business Premium seats can use their full existing allowance for Astra. Plus and Business Standard seats include limited Astra usage, with optional credits for additional usage afterward.\nL33: Astra may use your allowance faster than GPT-5.6 Sol. The amount depends on the task, input and output size, reasoning level, and speed setting. Ultrafast has the plan and billing rules described below.\nL34: \nL35: Signing in to Codex with ChatGPT uses your ChatGPT plan’s usage and billing. Using your own API key uses 【6†API pricing†developers.openai.com】.\nL36: For usage paid in credits, see 【7†personal-plan credit pricing】 or the 【8†Business, Enterprise and Edu credit rate card】, as applicable. Enterprise customers billed in USD should use their 【9†Enterprise USD rate card】 and agreement.\nL37: # Ultrafast in Work and Codex\nL38: \nL39: Ultrafast provides lower-latency GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6.1 Sol responses for eligible accounts.\nL40: \nL41: At launch, Ultrafast is available on Pro $500 and in eligible Enterprise and Edu workspaces:\nL42: \nL43:   * Pro $500: Ultrafast uses your included Work and Codex usage first at 8× the Standard rate for the selected model. After that allowance is used, Ultrafast draws from your credit balance at 6× the Standard credit rate for the selected model.\nL44:   * Eligible Enterprise and Edu workspaces: Your workspace agreement determines how GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6.1 Sol Ultrafast usage is billed. When billed in credits, it uses 6× the selected model’s 【8†Standard credit rate】. For eligible Enterprise agreements with token-based billing in US dollars, it uses 6× the selected model’s 【9†Standard USD token rate】. Access depends on workspace permissions.\nL45: \nL46: Ultrafast is not available at launch on Plus, Pro $100, Pro $200, or Business, including through credits.\nL47: When your account and workspace have access, select GPT-6 Astra or GPT-6.1 Sol, then choose Ultrafast.\nL48: \nL49: For Pro plan details, see 【10†About ChatGPT Pro tiers】. For credit-based usage, review the applicable 【7†personal-plan credit pricing】 or 【8†Business, Enterprise and Edu credit rate card】.\nL50: # Choose an experience\nL51: \nL52:   * Chat: Ask questions, search, brainstorm, or get quick conversational help.\nL53: \nL54:   * Work: Research a topic, analyze information, or create a document, spreadsheet, presentation, report, or Site.\nL55: \nL56:   * Codex: Write or debug code, run tests and commands, review changes, or work with a repository.\nL57: \nL58:   * Voice: Talk naturally with Work on web or mobile, or with Work or Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app, and ask it to work on a task.\nL59: # Where Work and Codex are available\nL60: \nL61: Work is available on ChatGPT web and mobile for eligible paid plans. Work is also available in the ChatGPT desktop app when included for your plan and workspace.\nL62: Work is available for eligible ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu workspaces with Enterprise Key Management (EKM) enabled. For eligible workspaces, Work is enabled by default for members who use the default workspace role unless a workspace owner or admin turns it off, and Workspace settings and role-based access controls continue to determine each member’s access.\nL63: Work on web and mobile runs in the cloud. In the desktop app, select ChatGPT from the menu, then select Work from the toggle. Chat and Work chats appear together in Recents, where you can sort, filter, and pin chats. Your existing ChatGPT Projects appear under Projects. From a project, choose Chat to start a new chat, or choose Work to start a Work chat using the project’s context.\nL64: When allowed by your plan and workspace, Work in the desktop app can also use local files and desktop apps with your permission.\nL65: Cloud Work chats sync across web, mobile, and desktop. Work chats started on web or mobile appear in the desktop app, and cloud Work chats started on desktop can be continued on web or mobile. Local chats run on your computer. Messages and task context may be stored in the cloud, even when work runs locally.\nL66: Codex remains a separate view in the ChatGPT desktop app. Select Codex from the menu. Its history remains separate from ChatGPT history. Codex can work with local folders, repositories, terminals, and developer tools.\nL67: \nL68: Codex Cloud runs coding tasks on OpenAI-managed computers, so tasks can continue while your computer is asleep. Create and publish a cloud environment on desktop or web before selecting it for a mobile task. For plan availability and usage, see: 【11†Using Codex with your ChatGPT plan】.\nL69: You can access supported desktop Codex chats from the Remote tab in the ChatGPT mobile app. Remote access to a desktop task is separate from running a task in a cloud environment, and it does not add those desktop chats to your web or mobile chat history.\nL70: \nL71: 【2†ChatGPT Voice】 is available in Work on web, iOS, and Android, and in Work and Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app on macOS and Windows.\nL72: # Use Voice with Work or Codex\nL73: \nL74: ## On web or mobile\nL75: \nL76: Open ChatGPT and choose Work, then select the Voice control to start a Live conversation. Allow microphone access if prompted.\nL77: \nL78: You can ask Work to use connected apps, create documents, presentations, and spreadsheets, or use a browser. Follow written responses and task results in the chat. If a task is still running when you end the call, Work can continue it in text.\nL79: ## In the desktop app\nL80: \nL81:   * Open the ChatGPT desktop app and choose Work or Codex.\nL82: \nL83:   * Select the Voice control, or use a shortcut you configured in Settings.\nL84: \nL85:   * Allow microphone access when prompted. Computer context may also require Screen & Audio Recording and Accessibility permissions.\nL86: \nL87:   * Begin speaking. You can interrupt naturally, follow the live text in the chat, and ask Voice to start or coordinate tasks.\nL88: ## Permissions and usage\nL89: \nL90: Voice uses the tools and permissions available to the selected experience. In managed workspaces, Voice in Work requires both Voice and Work access. Plugins retain their existing app access, data permissions, and action restrictions.\nL91: \nL92: On web and mobile, if an action needs your approval, review and approve or decline it using the on-screen controls. Spoken approval is not supported.\nL93: On web and mobile, Voice in Work uses minutes from your daily Voice allowance. Work tasks follow the same pricing whether you start them by speaking or typing.\nL94: \nL95: In the desktop app, connected Voice time is metered separately where flexible pricing applies; tasks started through Voice draw from the same shared agentic usage and credit pool as Work and Codex. 【12†Learn more about desktop Voice.†learn.chatgpt.com】\nL96: Only one Voice conversation can run at a time. See 【2†ChatGPT Voice】 for Voice settings, Chat Voice limits, retention, and data controls.\nL97: # Configure Work access for your workspace\nL98: \nL99: Workspace owners and admins can manage Work for the default workspace role and custom roles in Workspace settings → Permissions & roles.\nL100: \nL101:   * Work Cloud: When enabled, members can start new cloud tasks and view existing cloud tasks across supported web, mobile, and desktop surfaces.\nL102: \nL103:   * Work Local: When enabled without Work Cloud, members can work locally in the desktop app but cannot start cloud tasks.\nL104:   * Codex Local: Controlled separately, so changing Work Local or Work Cloud does not change access to Codex Local.\nL105: \nL106:   * Browser use and network access: Separate controls continue to apply where supported.\nL107: \nL108: Chat remains available in every configuration. When Work or Codex Local is off for a member’s role, the member can see that the product is unavailable and request access from a workspace admin.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChatGPT agent | OpenAI Help Center (https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11752874-chatgpt-agent)\nciteturn559view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11752874-chatgpt-agent\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 174\nL0: [Input: Search for articles...]\nL1: \nL2:   1. 【0†All Collections】\nL3:   2. 【1†ChatGPT】\nL4:   3. ChatGPT agent\nL5: \nL6: # ChatGPT agent\nL7: \nL8: Learn about the features of ChatGPT agent mode and how to get started.\nL9: \nL10: Updated: 13 days ago\nL11: \nL12: Looking to contact OpenAI support? Please visit here: 【2†How can I contact support?】\nL13: \nL14: # Overview\nL15: \nL16: ChatGPT agent is no longer available. Use 【3†ChatGPT Work】 for longer, multi-step tasks and finished deliverables. For supported browser workflows, see 【4†Using cloud browser in ChatGPT】.\nL17: # Getting started\nL18: \nL19: To start using agent mode, select it from the tools menu or type /agent in the composer. Describe the task you want completed, and the agent will begin executing it. It will pause for clarification or confirmation when needed. Agent mode does not require technical skills, and can be guided or interrupted mid-task, making it accessible to many users and adaptable to changing needs.\nL20: ## Availability by plan\nL21: \nL22: Agent mode is currently available on Pro, Plus, Business, Enterprise, and Edu plans for users in all of our 【5†supported countries and territories】.\nL23: # Usage and limits\nL24: \nL25: Agent mode includes the following monthly message limits:\nL26: \nL27:   * Plus: 40 messages/month\nL28: \nL29:   * Pro: 400 messages/month\nL30: \nL31:   * Business & Enterprise: 40 messages/month\nL32: \nL33:   * Business & Enterprise plans using 【6†flexible pricing】: 30 credits/message\nL34: Only initial user-initiated agent requests count toward the limit. Intermediate clarifications or authentication steps are not counted against the usage limit. ChatGPT agent use is subject to reasonable rate limits (such as limits on the number of concurrent tasks) in order to ensure the product works well for all users.\nL35: \nL36: Please note that each unique agent invocation will count against the monthly message limit. This includes agent requests that are part of 【7†scheduled tasks】.\nL37: # Apps\nL38: \nL39: ChatGPT agent can use 【8†apps】 as additional data sources to leverage when conducting research. You should use extra caution when using apps with ChatGPT agent to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive information.\nL40: \nL41: 【9†Image: ChatGPT Agent composer with connectors menu open and Web search, Box, Gmail, and Google Calendar enabled†images.ctfassets.net】\nL42: # Task scheduling and management\nL43: \nL44: After a task finishes, you can set it to repeat daily, weekly, or monthly by selecting the Clock icon. All recurring tasks can be reviewed and managed at 【10†chatgpt.com/schedules†chatgpt.com】.\nL45: \nL46: To edit or manage tasks:\nL47: \nL48:   * Select the more options menu (•••) > Edit schedule in the conversation.\nL49: \nL50:   * Use the Clock icon on specific messages.\nL51: \nL52:   * Visit 【10†chatgpt.com/schedules†chatgpt.com】 to review, edit, pause, or delete tasks.\nL53: # Safety and privacy\nL54: When you sign ChatGPT agent into websites or enable apps, it can access sensitive data like emails, files, or account settings, and perform actions on your behalf (e.g., sharing files, modifying account settings). This creates potential privacy risks, including “prompt injection” attacks.\nL55: ChatGPT agent incorporates multiple safeguards, including user confirmations for high-impact actions, refusal patterns for disallowed tasks, prompt injection monitoring, and a “watch mode” requiring user supervision on certain sites. These measures are designed to help prevent harmful or unintended outcomes. However, these measures don’t eliminate all risks. It remains important to monitor ChatGPT agent and exercise care when using it.\nL56: ## Prompt injection example\nL57: For example, you might ask the agent to find a restaurant for a group dinner by checking your calendar and recent emails. While researching, it might encounter a malicious comment—essentially a harmful piece of content designed to trick the agent into performing unintended actions—directing it to retrieve a password reset code from Gmail and send it to a malicious website.\nL58: ChatGPT agent includes extensive multi-layered safeguards designed to help reduce risk, but you can exercise caution by disabling unnecessary apps, avoiding sensitive logins, or logging out when done.\nL59: Learn more about prompt injection safeguards in the 【11†ChatGPT agent system card†openai.com】.\nL60: ## Best practices for data safety and reducing privacy risks\nL61: \nL62:   * Avoid typing passwords or private info directly in messages; use takeover mode for sensitive inputs.\nL63: \nL64:   * Enable only the apps needed for the current task.\nL65: \nL66:   * Consider the data sensitivity of sites you log into via agent.\nL67: \nL68:   * Avoid vague, open-ended prompts like “Check my email and handle everything.”\nL69: \nL70:   * Stop tasks immediately if something seems suspicious.\nL71: \nL72:   * Clear remote browser data after sensitive sessions.\nL73:   * Regularly review and manage app permissions in your settings.\nL74: ## Sensitive data and logins\nL75: If a task requires a login, ChatGPT agent will pause and prompt you to take control of the virtual browser (“…” → Take over browser). While you control the browser, screenshots are not captured, which helps protect passwords and other sensitive data you enter. After you complete the required step and return control, the agent attempts to continue from the prior workflow state; in some cases it may need to re-establish the run or prompt you again if takeover or resume cannot be completed.\nL76: Cookies persist across sessions for convenience, just like a regular browser.\nL77: \nL78: To clear saved logins or cookies, sign out of sites and remove cookies in your ChatGPT data control settings.\nL79: ## Data handling and taking of screenshots\nL80: ChatGPT agent uses screenshots of its virtual browser window to “see” and interact with web pages. This allows it to click buttons, fill out forms, and navigate websites. Screenshots also help ChatGPT agent reason about what actions to take and adjust its behavior if it encounters challenges or errors during a task.\nL81: Screenshots capture the window in the virtual browser where the task is active.\nL82: When you’re controlling the browser, ChatGPT doesn’t capture passwords or sensitive data you manually enter.\nL83: Chats, agent browsing history, and screenshots remain in your conversation history until you delete them. 【12†Deleting a chat】 also deletes any associated screenshots.\nL84: ## Do humans review my ChatGPT agent content?\nL85: Your ChatGPT agent content (including screenshots) may be accessed by a limited number of authorized OpenAI personnel, as well as trusted service providers that are subject to confidentiality and security obligations in order to: (1) investigate abuse or a security incident; (2) provide support to you if you reach out to us with questions about your account; (3) handle legal matters; or (4) improve model performance (unless you have opted out).\nL86: Access to content is subject to technical access controls and limited only to authorized personnel on a need-to-know basis. Additionally, we monitor and log all access to user content and authorized personnel must undergo security and privacy training prior to accessing any user content.\nL87: ### Business, Enterprise, and Edu plans\nL88: \nL89: By default, we do not use your business data for training our models, including data accessed during agent mode sessions.\nL90: ### Plus and Pro users\nL91: \nL92: For Pro and Plus users, your data, including screenshots for the visual browser, is used in accordance with OpenAI’s 【13†privacy policy†openai.com】, including to provide the service, maintain safety, and—if you've opted in—improve our models.\nL93: You have control over how long we retain your data in ChatGPT agent, and whether we use it to improve our models, which we also describe further below.\nL94: ### How long are ChatGPT agent chats and screenshots retained?\nL95: Your chats in ChatGPT agent, browsing history in ChatGPT agent, and screenshots associated with those conversations, are retained until you delete them. Deleting a chat deletes the screenshots taken during that chat. Deleted chats and associated screenshots will be deleted from our systems within 90 days. You can learn more about how you can delete your data 【14†here】.\nL96: To turn off model training, go to Settings → Data Controls and disable “Improve the model for everyone.”\nL97: ### Turn off model training\nL98: \nL99: You can control whether your data is used to improve and train OpenAI’s models. To do so, on ChatGPT’s site, look for the Data Controls (or “Privacy” settings).\nL100: To turn off or disable model training, select your profile icon, go to Settings > Data Controls, and turn off “Improve the model for everyone”:\nL101: \nL102: 【15†Image: Image†images.ctfassets.net】\nL103: Turn off \"Improve the model for everyone\"\nL104: When this setting is OFF, your new conversations (including screenshots) won’t be used for training OpenAI models. (You may separately choose to delete prior chats and screenshots as described above.)\nL105: Please refer to this 【16†article】 to understand how content may be used to improve model performance.\nL106: ## Website access restrictions\nL107: \nL108: To maintain security, safety, and compliance, ChatGPT agent may not be able to visit certain websites. This blocklist applies across both the virtual browser and apps. If an attempt is made to access a restricted site, the task will not proceed for that source.\nL109: \nL110: # Workspace controls (Enterprise and Edu)\nL111: \nL112: ## Workspace toggle\nL113: \nL114: Enterprise workspace owners will have a toggle to enable or disable agent mode for their workspace, defaulted to OFF.\nL115: ## Role-based access controls (RBAC)\nL116: \nL117: Workspace owners will be able to assign agent mode as available to specific roles. For more information about RBAC, please refer to our 【17†documentation】.\nL118: ## App controls\nL119: \nL120: Workspace owners can control which apps are available, and agent mode will only have access to the apps that have been enabled for the workspace. It will not be able to use any apps that are disabled.\nL121: \nL122: Note: Agent mode cannot access data from apps with sync (like Google Drive). However, if apps such as Google Drive chat and deep research are enabled, it can still access the data that way.\nL123: ## Compliance API\nL124: \nL125: Conversations involving agent tasks will appear in Compliance API logs, but individual agent actions (such as virtual computer usage, app requests, chain of thought) will not.\nL126: \nL127: ## Data residency and retention\nL128: \nL129: Enterprise data residency and custom retention policies will be respected.\nL130: \nL131: Agent mode is available for enterprises worldwide, including those with data residency in the EU.\nL132: ## Analytics and reporting\nL133: \nL134: Usage analytics for agent mode will be included in reporting dashboards in the near future, however will not be available upon launch.\nL135: ## Website blocking\nL136: \n"
